IMPORT prelude
IMPORT daydate
IMPORT `cpp-core`
IMPORT `cp4-nouns`
IMPORT `cp4-disability-pension`

-- Tests of section 56 (disability pension). Every expected value is worked out by hand from the text of
-- section 56, as written beside the assertion. The Pension Index series and every earnings figure are
-- invented for the test, because they come from sections this piece cannot see.

§ `Tests -- section 56`

§§ `Fixtures`

-- A short Pension Index series, positionally 1986, 1987, 1988: 100, 103, 106.
`the test series` MEANS LIST 100, 103, 106

-- An earnings record, positionally: total pensionable earnings, months in the contributory period,
-- months deducted under 48(2) and (4), earnings attributable to the months deducted.
`earnings 120000 over 100 months` MEANS `cp4 Earnings record for a disability pension` OF 120000, 100, 0, 0
`earnings 120000 over 40 months` MEANS `cp4 Earnings record for a disability pension` OF 120000, 40, 0, 0
`earnings 120000 over 100 months with 10 months and 12000 deducted` MEANS `cp4 Earnings record for a disability pension` OF 120000, 100, 10, 12000
`earnings 120000 over 20 months` MEANS `cp4 Earnings record for a disability pension` OF 120000, 20, 0, 0

§§ `Section 56(2) -- the flat rate benefit`

-- (a): 1986 is $91.06, whatever the index.
#ASSERT `s 56(2) — the flat rate benefit of a disability pension` 1986 `the test series` EQUALS JUST 91.06
-- (b): 1987 is 233.38 times the Pension Index for 1987 over the Pension Index for 1986: 233.38 x 103/100 = 240.3814.
#ASSERT `s 56(2) — the flat rate benefit of a disability pension` 1987 `the test series` EQUALS JUST 240.3814
-- (c): 1988 is the 1987 amount times 106/103: 233.38 x 103/100 x 106/103 = 233.38 x 1.06 = 247.3828.
#ASSERT `s 56(2) — the flat rate benefit of a disability pension` 1988 `the test series` EQUALS JUST 247.3828
-- before 1986 the text gives no amount; a year the series does not reach is not decided either.
#ASSERT `s 56(2) — the flat rate benefit of a disability pension` 1985 `the test series` EQUALS NOTHING
#ASSERT `s 56(2) — the flat rate benefit of a disability pension` 1989 `the test series` EQUALS NOTHING

§§ `Section 56(4) and (4.1) -- average monthly pensionable earnings`

-- (4): 120000 over 100 months, the greater of 100 and 48 months: 1200.
#ASSERT `s 56(4) — the average monthly pensionable earnings of a contributor deemed disabled after 1997` `earnings 120000 over 100 months` EQUALS 1200
-- (4): 40 months is fewer than 48, so the divisor is 48: 120000 / 48 = 2500.
#ASSERT `s 56(4) — the average monthly pensionable earnings of a contributor deemed disabled after 1997` `earnings 120000 over 40 months` EQUALS 2500
-- (4), subject to 48(2) and (4): (120000 - 12000) / (100 - 10) = 108000 / 90 = 1200.
#ASSERT `s 56(4) — the average monthly pensionable earnings of a contributor deemed disabled after 1997` `earnings 120000 over 100 months with 10 months and 12000 deducted` EQUALS 1200
-- (4.1): 20 months is fewer than 24, so the divisor is 24: 120000 / 24 = 5000; 100 months: 1200.
#ASSERT `s 56(4.1) — the average monthly pensionable earnings of a contributor deemed disabled in 1997` `earnings 120000 over 20 months` EQUALS 5000
#ASSERT `s 56(4.1) — the average monthly pensionable earnings of a contributor deemed disabled in 1997` `earnings 120000 over 100 months` EQUALS 1200
-- (4): 40 months is above 24 but below 48, so 1997 and 1998 differ: 120000 / 40 = 3000 in 1997; 2500 after.
#ASSERT `s 56(4.1) — the average monthly pensionable earnings of a contributor deemed disabled in 1997` `earnings 120000 over 40 months` EQUALS 3000

§§ `Section 56(3), (3.1) and (3.2) -- the three portions`

-- (3): 25% x 1200 = 300 for a contributor deemed disabled in 1998 or later.
#ASSERT `s 56(3) — the base portion of the contributor's retirement pension` 1998 `earnings 120000 over 100 months` EQUALS JUST 300
#ASSERT `s 56(3) — the base portion of the contributor's retirement pension` 2020 `earnings 120000 over 100 months` EQUALS JUST 300
-- in 1997 subsection (4.1) is used: 25% x 5000 = 1250 for 20 months.
#ASSERT `s 56(3) — the base portion of the contributor's retirement pension` 1997 `earnings 120000 over 20 months` EQUALS JUST 1250
-- 1996: neither (4) nor (4.1) applies, and the text gives nothing.
#ASSERT `s 56(3) — the base portion of the contributor's retirement pension` 1996 `earnings 120000 over 100 months` EQUALS NOTHING
-- (3.1): 8.33% x 1000 = 83.3. (3.2): 33.33% x 1000 = 333.3.
#ASSERT `s 56(3.1) — the first additional portion of the contributor's retirement pension` 1000 EQUALS 83.3
#ASSERT `s 56(3.2) — the second additional portion of the contributor's retirement pension` 1000 EQUALS 333.3

§§ `Section 56(4.01) and (4.02) -- first and second additional monthly pensionable earnings`

-- 400 months is at most 480: 240000 / 480 = 500.
#ASSERT `s 56(4.01) — the first additional monthly pensionable earnings` 400 240000 999999 EQUALS 500
-- exactly 480 months is still "less than or equal to 480": 240000 / 480 = 500.
#ASSERT `s 56(4.01) — the first additional monthly pensionable earnings` 480 240000 999999 EQUALS 500
-- 481 months exceeds 480: the aggregate of the 480 highest (288000) / 480 = 600.
#ASSERT `s 56(4.01) — the first additional monthly pensionable earnings` 481 240000 288000 EQUALS 600
#ASSERT `s 56(4.02) — the second additional monthly pensionable earnings` 100 48000 999999 EQUALS 100
#ASSERT `s 56(4.02) — the second additional monthly pensionable earnings` 481 48000 96000 EQUALS 200

§§ `Section 56(5) -- the contributory period`

-- Born 2 February 1940: 18 in February 1958, before 1966, so the period starts in January 1966; it ends
-- with December 1990. January 1966 to December 1990 is 25 years, 300 months, both ends counted.
#ASSERT `s 56(5) — the number of months in the contributory period` (YMD 1940 2 2) (YMD 1990 12 1) (YMD 1991 6 1) 0 0 EQUALS 300
-- Born 10 May 1970: 18 on 10 May 1988, deemed reached on 1 June 1988 (section 2(2)); the period runs from
-- June 1988 to March 2010: 21 years and 10 months, 262 months, less 10 excluded for disability and 5
-- family allowance months (the benefit month is after 1977): 247.
#ASSERT `s 56(5) — the number of months in the contributory period` (YMD 1970 5 10) (YMD 2010 3 1) (YMD 2010 4 1) 10 5 EQUALS 247
-- Born 2 February 1940, disabled in December 1976: January 1966 to December 1976 is 11 years, 132 months;
-- less 10 months excluded for disability = 122. Paragraph (d) bites only for a benefit payable for a month
-- after December 1977, so a benefit month of December 1977 does not subtract the 5 family allowance months.
#ASSERT `s 56(5) — the number of months in the contributory period` (YMD 1940 2 2) (YMD 1976 12 1) (YMD 1977 12 1) 10 5 EQUALS 122
-- A benefit month of January 1978 is after December 1977, so the 5 are subtracted: 132 - 10 - 5 = 117.
#ASSERT `s 56(5) — the number of months in the contributory period` (YMD 1940 2 2) (YMD 1976 12 1) (YMD 1978 1 1) 10 5 EQUALS 117
-- A disability month before the start of the period leaves no months at all.
#ASSERT `s 56(5) — the number of months in the contributory period` (YMD 1970 5 10) (YMD 1985 1 1) (YMD 1986 1 1) 0 0 EQUALS 0

§§ `Section 56(6) -- if a division of unadjusted pensionable earnings occurs`

-- Before the division the portion was 300, after it 200; no months excluded, 100 months remain until age 65,
-- and 100 months in the contributory period: (300 x (0 + 100) + 200 x 100) / (0 + 100 + 100) = 50000/200 = 250.
#ASSERT `s 56(6) — the base portion of the retirement pension when a division reduces the disability pension` TRUE 300 200 0 100 100 EQUALS JUST 250
-- With 10 months excluded: (300 x 110 + 200 x 100) / (110 + 100) = 53000/210.
#ASSERT `s 56(6) — the base portion of the retirement pension when a division reduces the disability pension` TRUE 300 200 10 100 100 EQUALS JUST (53000 DIVIDED BY 210)
-- If the division does not reduce the disability pension, subsection (6) does not give an amount.
#ASSERT `s 56(6) — the base portion of the retirement pension when a division reduces the disability pension` FALSE 300 200 0 100 100 EQUALS NOTHING
-- A zero divisor is not answered either.
#ASSERT `s 56(6) — the base portion of the retirement pension when a division reduces the disability pension` TRUE 300 200 0 0 0 EQUALS NOTHING

§§ `Section 56(1) -- the basic monthly amount`

-- Flat rate 100, portions 300, 80 and 320: 100 + 75% x 700 = 100 + 525 = 625.
#ASSERT `s 56(1) — the basic monthly amount of a disability pension` 100 (`cp4 Retirement pension portions` OF 300, 80, 320) EQUALS 625
-- No retirement pension portions at all leaves the flat rate benefit alone.
#ASSERT `s 56(1) — the basic monthly amount of a disability pension` 91.06 (`cp4 Retirement pension portions` OF 0, 0, 0) EQUALS 91.06
